	<font face="Arial" size="+1"><B>UO2 Unveiled-What We Know </B></font><p>
<!-- Story -->
<font face="Arial" size="-1" color="black">"Blending the grand traditional history of the venerable Ultima universe with a whole new twist of time lines, a jaw-dropping 3D graphics and animation overhaul, and the lessons learned from five years of development and game support in this field, this experienced UO2 team has set its stall on pushing the envelope for the genre it pioneered." -<a class=post href="http://www.pcgamer.com/">PC Gamer</a>
<p>
Thus we are introduced to UO2 in the first of what will be many features on this upcoming title.  But what do we know about this game and what's in store for the new crop of residents in Britannia?  We must start from the beginning with the history of UO2.
<p>
The best place to find out about the history of UO2 is directly at the source, <a class=post href="http://www.uo2.com" 
     >the Official Site</a>.  Here you may find the legends behind the <a class=post  href="http://www.uo2.com/legends-prequel_1.html" 
     >Britannians</a>, the <a class=post href="http://www.uo2.com/legends-meer_1.html" 
     >Meer</a> and the <a class=post href="http://www.uo2.com/legends-juka_1.html" 
     >Juka</a>.  The Meer are an ancient race of cat-like people swathed in magic while the Juka are a slave race from the future.  A "Great Cataclysm" has ripped the timeline asunder, diverging the world from that of Ultima Online and bringing these two races together with the humans.  UO2 takes place 200 years after this Cataclysm.  <a class=post href="http://www.pcgamer.com/">PC Gamer</a> confirms that there will indeed be three full continents in the game, Britannia for the humans, Logosia for the Jukas and Avenosh for the Meer.
<p>
In the <a class=post href="http://www.uo2.com/legends-prequel_1.html" 
     >Britannia fiction</a> we are just being introduced to the source of the Cataclysm, presumably a spell planned by Nystul and Lord British.  Lord Blackthorn sees the future and adamantly opposes what he sees.  <a class=post href="http://www.pcgamer.com/">PC Gamer</a> reveals that we may expect to see these characters once again however Lord Blackthorn is now a "psychotic cybernetic loon" who resides in Logos, the home city of the Juka.
<p>
So what about the game engine itself?  Richard Garriott, before leaving Origin, made this quote concerning the engine itself and what may be expected:
<blockquote>Ah... Well we have taken a similar approach to what we did with Ultima: Ascension, but with better learning. The world will ultimately have weather, day/night etc. As the world will be populated with real people we will have less issues with AI then we had in Ultima: Ascension. Yet still we have a much better plan for Ultima Online 2 in how NPC's and Creatures will operate. The world is also a much better designed 3D landscape and thus has better interactions than Ultima: Ascension with the environment itself. The interface is kinda cool too. Much more intuitive than before..."</blockquote>
Does that hold true as to what the folks at <a class=post href="http://www.pcgamer.com/">PC Gamer</a> learned?  In short, yes.  First, Origin has declared several times now that is it brand new code, not revamped Ultima Ascension code.  Also, the engine will take advantage of such features as bump-mapping.  While not from the game engine per se, <a class=post href="http://www.shugashack.com/pic.x/misc/042800_uo2_1.jpg" 
     >this shot</a> from a GeForce2 GTS presentation courtesy of the <a class=post href="http://www.shugashack.com" 
     >ShugaShack</a> is very close to what UO2 will look 
      like. in fact, Cynthe of Origin made this comment about the shot: 
      <BLOCKQUOTE>The shot released on Friday is an image taken 
      from a marketing spot created by one of our 3D artists for nVidia. The 
      models, textures, backgrounds and particle effects are straight from UO2, 
      and are consistent with the ones that we will be showing at E3. Since the 
      current build of the UO2 engine does not yet support particle effects or 
      bump-mapping, we chose to render the scene using actual in-game art. Also, 
      we added the bump-mapping as it will be done in the game. Thus, while the 
      shot provides an accurate indication of the beauty in UO2, it was not a 
      "screenshot" taken directly from the game's engine.
      </BLOCKQUOTE>
The primary point of view will be behind-the-head in a third-person type of view as stated by Ubiq:
<blockquote>UO2 will feature a 'behind-the-head' cam, with some level of adjustable zoom. We are looking at other possible camera settings, including fully adjustable, 1st person, and UO-style isometric, but I cannot 100% guarantee you at this time that we will solve all of the problems to make these viewpoints feel as smooth and as polished as the behind-cam, and publishing a stable and polished product will always be the UO2 team's number one priority. </blockquote>
And the interface itself, as alluded to by Garriott, will indeed be very intuitive, borrowing a great deal from the Windows interface.  For instance, one may right-click to bring up an options menu and the X in the upper right corner of boxes may be used to close them.  In addition, there will be a scroll bar to dock commonly used commands to ease access to them.
<p>
The big issue though, as is the mainstay of any Ultima, is customization.  Cynthe said, "Basically, we agree that making your character look as unique as possible is one of the best ways to really get into the game, and that's something we really want to see happen," and it looks like that's exactly what they're doing.  The <a class=post href="http://www.pcgamer.com/">PC Gamer</a> exclusive mentions the ability to customize skin hue, hair color, tattoos and scars.  The skill system, not a use-based system but instead using points allocated to skills as you "level", also allows a great deal of customization and features over 1000 branching options.  What are these skill trees?  Ubiq answers in the following way:
<blockquote>Skill trees start from a small number (currently about a dozen) boxes, and expand and unfold quickly as you advance. Fully expanded, our skill trees are large enough and numerous enough to wallpaper a room - I know this because Mobius has done so. Each skill tree has numerous branches, which offer different sub abilities. Some skill trees are quite large (the combat one currently has 150+ skills in it), whereas others are quite small (there are two skill trees that only have two boxes in them). We're talking a lot of freakin' skills here. 
<p>
It is difficult enough to simply implement all of the ways that these skills can function, advance, and interact. Ensuring that each skill can only advance on use of that skill quickly becomes a technical infeasibility, a data storage monstrosity and a design nightmare. It also becomes difficult to add new things, and hard to balance. Given the options, we had a couple of choices: <br>
* Reduce the number of cool skills that players can learn and perform, but allow them to advance only by doing them.<br>
* Put in a simple-to-use, hard-to-exploit advancement system, and then put in a ton of freakin' skills. 
<p>Now then, if you ask me what makes for a better 
        game, a more immersive world, and a more complete community, it is one 
        that offers more skills and ways to grow and expand those skills, and by 
        extension, greater character customization.</p>
                                         
</blockquote>Also, every 
      time you 
"level up" and earn more skill points you can allocate some already used ones into news areas, a sort of skill point recycling system. However, such recycling is  limited as Ubiq stated, "The rate that you can unlearn a skill is controlled, to prevent a blacksmith from becoming a warrior overnight." An added little  bonus to the system is that certain skills can be made extremely hard to find by hiding the teacher for them. You can bet  the very best skills will require teachers in very hard to reach places. Ubiq stated of  this skill system, "We actually think that it is one of the most solid, well-thought out parts of UO2's design, and at this stage we feel like it. s the best total advancement system that we've ever seen in a MMORPG."
<p>
Combat will hopefully be one of the most exciting parts of the game and Origin is hard at work trying to make melee just as fun as magic.  Magic users will have ten schools to play with and more than 100 spells according to <a class=post href="http://www.pcgamer.com/" 
     >PC Gamer</a>.  How can you beat that?  Mobius has a plan:
<blockquote>The large amount of time I spent working on the DragonRealms' combat system has definitely had some influence on my design of UO2. Expect to see the strategy making a big comeback. In the past, pure warrior professions tended to get boring because combat usually consisted of clicking a critter and watching its health go down. Spell-casters, on the other hand, tend to be more exciting in battle since they have a spiffy array of spells from which to choose. 
<p>I don't see any reason why spell-casters should 
        have all the fun. Therefore, I want to enable experienced warriors to 
        possess a nice array of flashy attack maneuvers that sets them apart 
        from other professions. Since every maneuver has its strengths and 
        weaknesses, choosing the appropriate one for a given situation gives 
        combat a strong strategic aspect.</p>
                                                        </blockquote>
According to <a class=post href="http://www.pcgamer.com/">PC Gamer</a>, they have created an impressive combat system where you can create your own combo out of a group of strokes available through weapon specialization. Each of these has their own attack and defend so indeed, you will have to pick the right one for the situation.  The new AI won't be helping you any as it borrows from the pathfinding in Dungeon Keeper 2 to ensure they give you a run for your money.
<p>
With combat comes death and with death comes pkilling.  Pkilling will be dealt with by the use of "safe zones" according to the <a class=post href="http://www.uo2.com/faq.html">Official FAQ</a>.  <a class=post href="http://www.pcgamer.com/" 
     >PC Gamer</a> found out that the entire continent of Britannia, about a third of the world, will be considered a safe zone.  Outside these, a pkilling incident may go as follows:
<ul><li>The pkiller knocks his victim unconscious and receives an option to kill him or not
<li>If the pkiller decides not to kill his victim, he receives experience and a single item from his victim
<li>If the pkiller decide to kill his victim, he 
        gets all the victim's items but no experience and loses a lot of 
        reputation</li>                      </ul>
Death from monsters will involve loss of items and/or experience if it happens too often and expect some corpse hunts against the clock.  Some characters will also have access to a "bless" spell which ensures an item stays with them.
<p>
All in all, UO2 looks to be shaping up to be a great game.  We can expect it out in spring of 2001 with beta testing occurring later this year.  In addition to the game, expect <a class=post href="http://www.uo2.com/todd_1.html">toys by McFarlane</a>, fiction books from Simon and Schuster's Pocket Books, an anime movie, pewter collectibles from  Rawcliffe company, swords and armor from Museum Replicas and the standard trinkets such as t-shirts, mugs, etc.
</font></p>

</td></tr></table>

